# Charter tour

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:charter_agreement_protocol, "~> 0.6.0"},
  {:charter_agreement_signer, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## What this notebook does

Two parties set up a charter. They publish party descriptors, agree on a charter revision
and accept it. Then the issuer records a receipt for one action.

The notebook plays two roles:

* **The producer.** Each party signs its own artifacts with
  [charter_agreement_signer](https://hex.pm/packages/charter_agreement_signer), the way an
  application does.
* **The counterparty.** Everything after signing is verified with
  `charter_agreement_protocol` alone. A verifier never needs the signer or any private key.

The keys are generated from fixed seeds so every run prints the same digests. They are demo
keys; never use them for anything real.

## Check what this runtime can verify

An application calls `capabilities/0` once at boot. It reports, for each signature algorithm
in the registry, whether this Erlang runtime can verify it, and which crypto library is
linked.

```elixir
alias CharterAgreementProtocol, as: CAP
alias CharterAgreementProtocol.{Digest, Limits}

CAP.capabilities().algorithms
```

A row with `verifiable: false` means this runtime could not verify that algorithm's
pinned test signature: the linked crypto library lacks the algorithm, or offers it but
the check fails. Artifacts signed with it then fail with
`:algorithm_unsupported_on_substrate`, which says the signature could not be checked.
Check this at boot instead of finding out on the first artifact.

## A demo key handle

The signer never holds a private key. You hand it a key handle, `{module, ref}`, where the
module implements two callbacks:

* `sign/2` takes the exact bytes to sign and returns the raw signature.
* `key_identity/1` returns the key id and public key in one call.

In production the module talks to an HSM or a key service. This one keeps an Ed25519 key in
memory, which is fine for a demo:

```elixir
defmodule DemoKey do
  @behaviour CharterAgreementSigner

  def new(kid, seed_byte) do
    {public, private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519, :binary.copy(<<seed_byte>>, 32))
    %{kid: kid, public: public, private: private}
  end

  @impl true
  def sign(message, key), do: {:ok, :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, message, [key.private, :ed25519])}

  @impl true
  def key_identity(key), do: {:ok, {key.kid, key.public}}
end

issuer_key = DemoKey.new("issuer-key", 1)
acceptor_key = DemoKey.new("acceptor-key", 2)

[issuer_key.kid, acceptor_key.kid]
```

The cell returns only the key ids, so no private key is printed.

## Each party publishes a descriptor

A party descriptor lists the keys a party signs with. Its content digest becomes the
party's identifier, and charters name parties by that digest.

```elixir
descriptor_claims = fn key ->
  %{
    "protocol_revision" => 2,
    "descriptor_number" => 1,
    "verification_keys" => [
      %{
        "key_id" => key.kid,
        "algorithm" => "Ed25519",
        "public_key" => Base.url_encode64(key.public, padding: false),
        "status" => "active"
      }
    ],
    "attestation_hints" => [],
    "extensions" => %{"critical" => %{}, "optional" => %{}},
    "effective_from" => "2026-08-25T10:00:00Z"
  }
end

{:ok, %{descriptor: issuer_descriptor}} =
  CharterAgreementSigner.sign_descriptor(descriptor_claims.(issuer_key), {DemoKey, issuer_key})

{:ok, %{descriptor: acceptor_descriptor}} =
  CharterAgreementSigner.sign_descriptor(
    descriptor_claims.(acceptor_key),
    {DemoKey, acceptor_key}
  )

String.slice(issuer_descriptor, 0, 60) <> "..."
```

The result is a compact JWS: three base64url parts joined by dots. The signer already
verified it with CAP before returning it, so a broken handle or a wrong key never produces
an artifact.

`protocol_revision` 2 with `Ed25519` is the default pair the signer mints. Pass
`algorithm: "ML-DSA-65"` with `protocol_revision` 3 for a post-quantum key instead.

## The counterparty verifies both descriptors

From here on, read each cell as the other side. It received two descriptors and checks each
party's key history on its own:

```elixir
{:ok, issuer_chain} = CAP.verify_descriptor_chain([issuer_descriptor], Limits.default())
{:ok, acceptor_chain} = CAP.verify_descriptor_chain([acceptor_descriptor], Limits.default())

party_digest = fn compact ->
  {:ok, descriptor} = CAP.decode_party_descriptor(compact, Limits.default())
  CAP.descriptor_digest(descriptor)
end

issuer_digest = party_digest.(issuer_descriptor)
acceptor_digest = party_digest.(acceptor_descriptor)

%{
  issuer: %{topology: issuer_chain.topology, digest: issuer_digest},
  acceptor: %{topology: acceptor_chain.topology, digest: acceptor_digest}
}
```

`:linear` means each history has one path from its first descriptor to its latest. Each
digest is that party's identifier.

## Write the charter revision

A charter revision holds the agreed terms. It is not signed. It is canonical JSON, and the
parties sign acceptances of its digest. `encode_charter_revision/1` turns a plain claims map
into those canonical bytes and checks them with the same decoder a verifier uses.

The legal text itself stays outside the charter; the revision carries its digest. The
`abp_bindings` entry names the deployment the issuer's agent runs, with example digests from
the Agent Blueprint Protocol.

```elixir
legal_text_digest =
  :legal_text |> Digest.hash("Supplier terms\n") |> Digest.to_tagged()

deployment_digest = "sha-256:tWFr0caS0AWFJd2UcB9gZv3kNjIUP8xZ08WWM_h8xgo"

revision_claims = %{
  "protocol_revision" => 2,
  "revision_number" => 1,
  "parties" => [
    %{"party_descriptor_digest" => issuer_digest, "role" => "issuer"},
    %{"party_descriptor_digest" => acceptor_digest, "role" => "acceptor"}
  ],
  "legal_text" => %{
    "content_digest" => legal_text_digest,
    "media_type" => "text/plain",
    "uri_hint" => "https://example.com/supplier-charter.txt"
  },
  "precedence_declaration" => "legal_text_governs",
  "attribution_declaration" => %{"basis" => "bound_deployments"},
  "termination_rules" => %{"reason_codes" => ["mutual", "breach"]},
  "abp_bindings" => [
    %{
      "party_role" => "issuer",
      "blueprint_id" => "example.demo/echo",
      "release_number" => 1,
      "content_digest" => "sha-256:b1Aw4cU5AbV9k8bdbZkRCsySDHGpTAwB-aQm57Wh7B8",
      "deployment_digest" => deployment_digest
    }
  ],
  "receipt_profile" => "com.example.charter/default",
  "extensions" => %{"critical" => %{}, "optional" => %{}},
  "effective_from" => "2026-08-25T12:00:00Z"
}

{:ok, revision_bytes} = CAP.encode_charter_revision(revision_claims)
{:ok, revision} = CAP.decode_charter_revision(revision_bytes, Limits.default())
charter_id = CAP.revision_digest(revision)
```

This is revision number 1, so it has no `charter_id` field. Its own digest becomes the
charter's id, and every later revision, acceptance and receipt carries that id.

## Both parties accept

Before it signs an acceptance, the producer checks the claims against the artifacts the
signing party holds. You pass that view as an artifact set built with `build_set/4`. If the
claims contradict it, for example by naming the wrong digest, the producer refuses and the
key is never used.

```elixir
{:ok, set} = CAP.build_set([revision_bytes], [], [], [issuer_descriptor, acceptor_descriptor])

acceptance_claims = fn digest, role ->
  %{
    "protocol_revision" => 2,
    "charter_id" => charter_id,
    "revision_number" => 1,
    "revision_digest" => charter_id,
    "party_descriptor_digest" => digest,
    "party_role" => role,
    "accepted_at" => "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"
  }
end

{:ok, %{acceptance: issuer_acceptance}} =
  CharterAgreementSigner.sign_acceptance(
    acceptance_claims.(issuer_digest, "issuer"),
    {DemoKey, issuer_key},
    set
  )

{:ok, %{acceptance: acceptor_acceptance}} =
  CharterAgreementSigner.sign_acceptance(
    acceptance_claims.(acceptor_digest, "acceptor"),
    {DemoKey, acceptor_key},
    set
  )

acceptances = [issuer_acceptance, acceptor_acceptance]
length(acceptances)
```

Two acceptances, one per party, both over the same revision digest.

## The counterparty verifies the charter

`verify_chain/5` takes everything the counterparty holds: revisions, acceptances,
descriptors and termination notices (none here). It returns chain facts, a struct that
prints as `<redacted>` so it never leaks into logs by accident. Read its fields directly:

```elixir
descriptors = [issuer_descriptor, acceptor_descriptor]

{:ok, chain_facts} =
  CAP.verify_chain([revision_bytes], acceptances, descriptors, [], Limits.default())

%{
  charter_id: chain_facts.charter_id,
  chain_topology: chain_facts.chain_topology,
  accepted_revision_digests: chain_facts.accepted_revision_digests,
  acceptances: length(chain_facts.acceptance_facts)
}
```

The revision is in `accepted_revision_digests` because both parties signed an acceptance of
its exact digest. One accepted path means the topology is `:linear`.

## Which revision governs?

Charters change over time, so the question is always "which revision governs at this
instant?" Ask it with `governing_revision/2`:

```elixir
%{
  before_effective_from: CAP.governing_revision(chain_facts, ~U[2026-08-25 11:00:00Z]),
  at_effective_from: CAP.governing_revision(chain_facts, ~U[2026-08-25 12:00:00Z])
}
```

Before the revision's `effective_from`, nothing governs, so the answer is `{:ok, :none}`.
From that instant on, the answer is the revision's digest. A third answer, `{:ok, :contested}`,
appears when two accepted revisions compete. The [fork repair notebook](fork-repair.livemd)
shows that case.

## The issuer records a receipt

A receipt is a signed record that one action happened under the charter. The `grant`,
`decision` and `outcome` members are the host's own report about that action. The `"host"`
scheme means the reference is the host's own record id.

The signer takes the issuer's verified chain facts so it can check the receipt against them
before returning it.

```elixir
receipt_claims = %{
  "protocol_revision" => 2,
  "charter_id" => charter_id,
  "revision_number" => 1,
  "revision_digest" => charter_id,
  "issuing_party_role" => "issuer",
  "agent_party_role" => "issuer",
  "deployment_digest" => deployment_digest,
  "grant" => %{"scheme" => "host", "id" => "order-1042"},
  "invocation_id" => "123e4567-e89b-42d3-a456-426614174000",
  "decision" => "accepted",
  "outcome" => "effect_committed",
  "occurred_at" => "2026-08-25T12:00:01Z",
  "recorded_at" => "2026-08-25T12:00:02Z",
  "extensions" => %{"critical" => %{}, "optional" => %{}}
}

{:ok, %{receipt: receipt}} =
  CharterAgreementSigner.sign_receipt(receipt_claims, {DemoKey, issuer_key}, chain_facts)

String.slice(receipt, 0, 60) <> "..."
```

## The counterparty verifies the receipt

```elixir
{:ok, receipt_facts} = CAP.verify_receipt(receipt, chain_facts, Limits.default())

%{
  governing_match: receipt_facts.governing_match,
  chain_conflict: receipt_facts.chain_conflict,
  deployment_digest_matched: receipt_facts.deployment_digest_matched,
  signing_key_id: receipt_facts.signing_key_id,
  outcome: receipt_facts.outcome
}
```

What these fields say:

* `governing_match: :match`: the revision the receipt names is the one that governed at
  `occurred_at`.
* `chain_conflict: :none`: the receipt names no revision the counterparty has not seen.
* `deployment_digest_matched: true`: the deployment is the one the revision binds to the
  issuer.
* `signing_key_id`: the id of the issuer key that signed it, as listed in the issuer's
  descriptor.
* `outcome`: what the issuer claims happened. CAP checks the signature on that claim, not
  the claim itself.

## An edited receipt fails to verify

Suppose someone edits the receipt to claim the action had no effect. Decode the payload,
change the outcome, and verify the edited receipt:

```elixir
[header, payload, signature] = String.split(receipt, ".")

edited_payload =
  payload
  |> Base.url_decode64!(padding: false)
  |> String.replace("effect_committed", "no_effect")
  |> Base.url_encode64(padding: false)

edited_receipt = Enum.join([header, edited_payload, signature], ".")
CAP.verify_receipt(edited_receipt, chain_facts, Limits.default())
```

Verification returns a typed error with a stable `code` and the path of the part that
failed. It never returns partial facts.

## What the facts do not prove

Every facts struct carries a `not_verified` list. It names what CAP did not check and
leaves to your application:

```elixir
receipt_facts.not_verified
```

Every result includes these twelve atoms. Some add more: a receipt checked against a
revision alone, without the chain, also lists `:signature`.

CAP proved that the right keys signed these exact bytes and that the receipt fits the
charter the counterparty holds. It did not prove that
the action was allowed, that it really happened, that the terms were met, that the legal
text is valid, or that the counterparty's view is complete. CAP never authorizes anything.
Your application makes those calls.

Next: the [fork repair notebook](fork-repair.livemd) breaks a charter on purpose and repairs
it.
